---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card, Grid, Timeline, TimelineItem } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DeckSection from "@components/DeckSection.astro";
import { tomVanDeLogtDeck, achiIIesDeck } from "@data/decklists";
---

<Layout
    title="History - Replenish Guide"
    description="Explore the competitive evolution of the Replenish archetype in Magic: The Gathering. Trace its legacy from Type 2 dominance in 2000 to its modern engineering and championship adaptation in Premodern."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="History of the Deck"
        description="From dominating Type 2 (Standard) in 2000 to adapting through years of format bans, Replenish has over twenty years of history behind it. Here are the milestones that shaped the archetype."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/parallax_wave.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Parallax Wave"
        imageCaption="Parallax Wave by Greg Staples"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <div style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <Timeline>
            <TimelineItem title="1. The Standard Origins (2000)" titleTag="h2">
                <p>
                    Replenish first broke out at the 2000 World Championship,
                    where Hall of Famer Tom van de Logt took it to a Top 8
                    finish. This old Type 2 build used artifact ramp like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Sky Diamond">Sky Diamond</a
                    > and mana denial from <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                    > to slow opponents down. A tutor package of
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mystical Tutor">Mystical Tutor</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Enlightened Tutor">Enlightened Tutor</a
                    > kept it consistent game to game, while a single
                    copy of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Energy Field">Energy Field</a
                    > bought time against aggro while the deck
                    set up its combo.
                </p>

                <Card>
                    <div class="deck-header">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="deck-header-title">
                                2000 World Championship (Standard)
                            </h3>
                            <p class="deck-header-subtitle">
                                Played by Tom van de Logt
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <Grid column={{ md: 3 }} gap="lg">
                        {
                            tomVanDeLogtDeck.columns.map((col) => (
                                <div>
                                    {col.sections.map((sec) => (
                                        <DeckSection
                                            title={sec.title}
                                            items={sec.items}
                                        />
                                    ))}
                                </div>
                            ))
                        }
                    </Grid>
                </Card>
            </TimelineItem>

            <TimelineItem
                title="2. The Parallax Tide Era (Pre-Ban)"
                titleTag="h2"
            >
                <p>
                    As Premodern grew into a serious competitive format,
                    deckbuilders figured out that <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                    > could act as a one-sided Armageddon. Running a full four
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    >, you could activate Tide targeting five opposing lands,
                    then sacrifice Seal to destroy Tide <em
                        >in response to its own exile triggers</em
                    >. Since Tide left the battlefield before those triggers
                    resolved, its return-to-play clause resolved
                    while nothing had been exiled yet. Once the stack cleared,
                    the targeted lands were gone for good—no <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > required.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Backed by maindeck disruption like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    > to shut down utility lands, Parallax Tide Replenish was
                    a clear tier-1 deck right up until it got banned.
                </p>
                <Card>
                    <div class="deck-header">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="deck-header-title">
                                The Parallax Tide Lock
                            </h3>
                            <p class="deck-header-subtitle">
                                Piloted by AchiIIes (Pre-Ban)
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <Grid column={{ md: 3 }} gap="lg">
                        {
                            achiIIesDeck.columns.map((col) => (
                                <div>
                                    {col.sections.map((sec) => (
                                        <DeckSection
                                            title={sec.title}
                                            items={sec.items}
                                        />
                                    ))}
                                </div>
                            ))
                        }
                    </Grid>
                </Card>
            </TimelineItem>

            <TimelineItem title="3. The Post-Ban Resurgence" titleTag="h2">
                <p>
                    When format curator Martin Berlin banned Parallax
                    Tide to cut down on repetitive, non-interactive land locks
                    and its high win rates, a lot of players wrote the deck
                    off—figuring Replenish couldn't survive without
                    its main mana-denial combo.
                </p>
                <p>
                    But dedicated pilots adapted fast. Replenish
                    has always been a combo-control deck at heart, so
                    losing Tide just meant reworking those slots into
                    other locks and interaction. By moving the main
                    endgame piece to <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                    > and shoring up the maindeck with counters
                    like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                    >, the deck proved it didn't need early land
                    destruction to stay a tournament
                    heavyweight.
                </p>
                <p>
                    See the modern post-ban configuration in our <a href="/analysis" class="guide-link">Maindeck Analysis</a>, or explore proven tournament-winning builds on the <a href="/deck" class="guide-link">Tournament Lists</a> page.
                </p>
            </TimelineItem>
        </Timeline>
    </div>
</Layout>

<style>
    .deck-header {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: flex-start;
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }

    .deck-header-title {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        color: var(--w-color-primary-10);
    }

    .deck-header-subtitle {
        margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0;
        color: var(--w-color-primary-40);
    }
</style>
